ne and lasting rather than temporary longing for his lover. He answers her directly with the focal declaration as part of an extended account of his love.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression itself contains no undertaking. In the immediately following speech, the speaker presents Maria as the person with whom he intends to live for a long time and eventually die. Maria's tears and happiness provide positive emotional uptake of the overall speech, though the text gives no separate verbal acceptance or precise terms for that future commitment.\",\"recognised_work\":false,\"recognition_note\":\"I do not confidently recognise the work from the supplied passages.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly represents the declaration as sincere: the narrator says Maria understands that he speaks truth. Nevertheless, the focal phrase alone does not specify concrete obligations, and the supplied evidence does not fully define the practical terms of the couple's future arrangement.\"}"
